--- body ---
## Context

On 2026-09-23 in search-works-for-zotero, a session ran the full `make check`
(over ten minutes, pytest and a mutation gate) twice in one
morning on diffs that held no code: an evidence PR (#612, one JSON record and
one log) and a ruling PR (#613, DECISIONS.md, a new doc, five tickets). The
author: "WTF are we running make check for a DECISIONS-only ticket". Six of
the current `check` prerequisites can see such a diff; the rest cannot,
whatever it contains.

Five whys, short: the project rule says "`make check` green before any commit"
with no scope; the session's memory reinforced it (a note measured it against
an hour of `/gaze`, so ten minutes read as cheap); the Makefile offers one
monolithic target; it is monolithic on purpose, after a partial run that died
on its last step was read as green (that project's ticket 0498); and with no
CI the local gate is the only gate, so the rule was kept simple enough that
nobody skips it. Root cause: the gate is uniform across diffs and nothing
asks what a given diff can break. That is a harness question, not one
project's: every repo with a pre-commit gate meets it.

## Relevant files

- `rules/workflow.md` — where a resident rule on scoping the gate would live
- `skills/hunt/SKILL.md` step 10 and `rules/coding-python.md` — currently
  require the full `make check` before every PR; both must recognize the
  scoped result for a doc-only PR or the pilot will still run the full gate
- `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md`, `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — gates that quote a
  `make` target; 0320 already scaled `/gaze`'s reviewer battery to the diff
- a project Makefile, e.g. search-works-for-zotero's `check:` line — the
  per-project map from paths to targets

## Actions

1. A harness rule: run the checks the diff can move, not the whole gate; name
   the skipped targets wherever the result is quoted (PR page, commit
   message), so a scoped run can never be read as a full one.
2. A small harness script that reads a per-project path-to-target map,
   diffs against `origin/<default>`, and runs the union of targets; any
   unmapped path runs the full gate. Last line: the targets it skipped.
3. Pilot in search-works-for-zotero: docs and tickets → lint, figures, names,
   progress, tickets, ticket-logs; plugin docs and manifests add sitter-version
   and sitter-mutants. Plugin source code and anything else unmapped run the
   full `make check`, preserving the code-merge invariant below.
4. Wire the selector into `/hunt`'s pre-PR step and align the resident Python
   testing rule: a doc-only PR with a declared path map runs the selected
   targets, with the skipped list in the PR description; a code PR and any
   project without a map still run the full gate. Make `/verify-gate` and
   `/gaze` recognize the scoped result without mistaking it for a full run.

## Test

Red first: a diff touching only `DECISIONS.md` selects the six doc targets and
names every remaining declared target as skipped; a diff touching one `.py` file selects the full
gate; a path absent from the map selects the full gate. Exercise `/hunt`'s
pre-PR step for both doc-only and code diffs, so a direct `make check` there
cannot bypass the selector.

## Verification

- [ ] The three test diffs select what the Test section says.
- [ ] The skipped-targets line is present on every run ("skipped: none" on a full one).
- [ ] `/hunt` uses the scoped result before a mapped doc-only PR and the full
      gate before a code PR or in a project without a path map.

## Invariants

- The full gate stays one command and stays required before a code merge.
- A scoped run never prints a bare success: the skipped list is always there.

## Exit criteria

The rule is resident, the script ships with its tests, `/hunt` and the review
gates consume its result, and the pilot project uses it for its next doc-only
PR without also running the full gate.

## Related: the raid's agent-timeout breaker measures the gate, not the agent

Same root cause, other surface. On 2026-09-23 a `/raid` in
search-works-for-zotero killed its ticket-0820 executor at 17 minutes for "no
push within 10 minutes". The executor was not stalled: its worktree held a
coherent rename, and its last words were "the adherence self-gate". It was
running the gate `/hunt` requires, and that project's full `make check` takes
more than 10 minutes (`sitter-mutants` alone, cold). An executor that follows
the contract cannot push inside the breaker, so the breaker fires on
compliance, not on stalls. Switching the coder model would not change it.

The breaker must observe a gate separately from the executor's edits. Merely
resetting its clock on a commit or worktree change still kills a compliant
executor during a gate that takes more than 10 minutes and makes no edits.
The scoped gate reduces that exposure but does not eliminate it for code PRs.

In `skills/raid/SKILL.md` § Circuit breakers, use the last push or worktree
change for the ordinary 10-minute stall window. While the executor is running
the declared gate, suspend that window only for a bounded gate allowance:
confirm that the gate process is still alive, set a project-specific maximum
longer than its measured cold run, and kill or escalate when that maximum
expires. Resume the ordinary stall window when the gate exits. A WIP push
after the red test is a useful recovery checkpoint, not a substitute for the
gate allowance; it cannot prevent the next 10-minute timeout by itself.

Exit addition: a live gate lasting more than 10 minutes with no worktree edits
does not trigger the ordinary breaker, while a silent executor outside the
gate still does and a hung gate reaches its own bounded deadline.
